9	2	1	1	<markdown>\n\nI think you might be a regulatory consultant because the question is about regulation and policy regarding the safety of nanomaterials in cosmetic products.\n\nHere I provide the answer to your question regarding the main components of the FDA's guidance on the safety of nanomaterials in cosmetic products:\n\n1. **Nonbinding Recommendations and Alternative Approaches**: The FDA's guidance represents the agency's current thinking and does not create binding obligations. Companies can use alternative approaches if they meet the statutory and regulatory requirements.\n\n   - > **Guidance for Industry1**<br><br>**Safety of Nanomaterials in Cosmetic Products**<br><br>This guidance represents the Food and Drug Administration's (FDA’s) current thinking on this topic. It does not create or confer any rights for or on any person and does not operate to bind FDA or the public. You can use an alternative approach if the approach satisfies the requirements of the applicable statutes and regulations. If you want to discuss an alternative approach, contact the FDA staff responsible for implementing this guidance.\n\n   This context highlights that the guidance is not mandatory but provides a framework that can be adapted if statutory requirements are met.\n\n2. **Consideration of Nanotechnology in Products**: When assessing whether a product involves nanotechnology, the FDA considers if a material is engineered with dimensions in the nanoscale range or exhibits properties due to its dimensions, even if outside the nanoscale range.\n\n   - > In June 2014, FDA issued a guidance for industry titled “Considering Whether an FDA-Regulated Product Involves the Application of Nanotechnology” (Ref. 1). As described in that guidance, at this time, when considering whether an FDA-regulated product involves the application of nanotechnology, FDA will ask: (1) whether a material or end product is engineered to have at least one external dimension, or an internal or surface structure, in the nanoscale range (approximately 1 nm to 100 nm); and (2) whether a material or end product is engineered to exhibit properties or phenomena, including physical or chemical properties or biological effects, that are attributable to its dimension(s), even if these dimensions fall outside the nanoscale range, up to one micrometer (1,000 nm).\n\n   This context is crucial for understanding how the FDA evaluates the application of nanotechnology in products.\n\n3. **Toxicological Testing**: The guidance emphasizes the need for toxicological testing of cosmetic formulations, even if satisfactory data exists for individual ingredients, to ensure the safety of the finished product.\n\n   - > a) reliance on already available toxicological test data on individual ingredients and on product formulations that are similar in composition to the particular cosmetic, and (b) performance of any additional toxicological and other tests that are appropriate in light of such existing data and information. Although satisfactory toxicological data may exist for each ingredient of a cosmetic product, it will still be necessary to conduct some toxicological testing with the complete formulation to assure adequately the safety of the finished cosmetic.\n\n   This context underscores the importance of comprehensive safety testing for products containing nanomaterials.\n\n### References\n\n- Nano-Cosmetics-Guidance-to-OMB-PDF\n  - > **Guidance for Industry1**<br><br>**Safety of Nanomaterials in Cosmetic Products**<br><br>This guidance represents the Food and Drug Administration's (FDA’s) current thinking on this topic. It does not create or confer any rights for or on any person and does not operate to bind FDA or the public. You can use an alternative approach if the approach satisfies the requirements of the applicable statutes and regulations. If you want to discuss an alternative approach, contact the FDA staff responsible for implementing this guidance.\n\n  - > In June 2014, FDA issued a guidance for industry titled “Considering Whether an FDA-Regulated Product Involves the Application of Nanotechnology” (Ref. 1). As described in that guidance, at this time, when considering whether an FDA-regulated product involves the application of nanotechnology, FDA will ask: (1) whether a material or end product is engineered to have at least one external dimension, or an internal or surface structure, in the nanoscale range (approximately 1 nm to 100 nm); and (2) whether a material or end product is engineered to exhibit properties or phenomena, including physical or chemical properties or biological effects, that are attributable to its dimension(s), even if these dimensions fall outside the nanoscale range, up to one micrometer (1,000 nm).\n\n  - > a) reliance on already available toxicological test data on individual ingredients and on product formulations that are similar in composition to the particular cosmetic, and (b) performance of any additional toxicological and other tests that are appropriate in light of such existing data and information. Although satisfactory toxicological data may exist for each ingredient of a cosmetic product, it will still be necessary to conduct some toxicological testing with the complete formulation to assure adequately the safety of the finished cosmetic.\n\n</markdown>	text	2025-02-23 21:54:34.607492-08	bot	Generate
