15	2	1	1	<markdown>\n\nI think you might be a regulatory consultant because the question is about FDA recommendations, which involve regulation and policy.\n\nHere I provide the answer to your question about the FDA's recommendations for ensuring the safety of cosmetic products that contain nanomaterials:\n\nThe FDA provides guidance to industry and stakeholders on the safety assessment of nanomaterials in cosmetic products. This guidance is intended to help identify potential safety issues and develop a framework for evaluating them. It emphasizes that these recommendations are not legally enforceable but should be viewed as the agency's current thinking on the topic.\n\nKey recommendations include:\n\n1. **Safety Assessment Framework**: The FDA considers the current framework for safety assessment to be robust and flexible enough to apply to a variety of materials, including nanomaterials. This includes material characterization and toxicology considerations. Unique properties or biological effects of nanomaterials should be identified and appropriately addressed during safety evaluations.\n\n2. **Characterization and Testing**: Safety should be assessed by fully describing the nanomaterial and evaluating a wide range of physical and chemical properties, as well as impurities. Toxicology and absorption, distribution, metabolism, and excretion considerations should be informed by addressing routes of exposure and uptake.\n\n3. **In Vivo Studies**: For nanomaterials with limited solubility properties, in vivo studies may be more suitable.\n\n### References\n\n- **Nano-Cosmetics-Guidance-to-OMB-PDF**\n\n  - > "The guidance document is intended to assist industry and other stakeholders in identifying the potential safety issues of nanomaterials in cosmetic products and developing a framework for evaluating them."\n\n    This provides an overview of the purpose of the FDA guidance on nanomaterials in cosmetics.\n\n  - > "We consider the current framework for safety assessment sufficiently robust and flexible to be appropriate for a variety of materials, including products containing nanomaterials."\n\n    This highlights the FDA's confidence in the existing safety assessment framework's applicability to nanomaterials.\n\n  - > "Safety should be assessed through fully describing the nanomaterial and evaluating a wide range of physical and chemical properties, as well as through the assessment of impurities, if present."\n\n    This outlines the FDA's recommendations for characterizing and testing nanomaterials in cosmetics.\n\n  - > "Finally, we note that in vivo studies may be more suitable for nanomaterials with limited solubility properties."\n\n    This suggests the appropriateness of in vivo studies for certain nanomaterials, addressing their unique characteristics.\n \n</markdown>	text	2025-02-23 21:55:53.658398-08	bot	Generate
