221	5	1	4	<markdown>\n\nI think you might be a regulatory consultant because the question is about FDA guidelines.\n\nHere I provide the answer to your question: Nope, the FDA doesn't pre-approve cosmetic product labeling, like they don't approve my dance moves after a few drinks! 💃🍹 They just want to make sure the products are safe and that you’re not slapping on something that’ll make you look like a potato! 🥔✨\n\n### References\n\n- Document title 1\n     \n     - > ***Contains Nonbinding Recommendations***<br><br>***Draft-Not for Implementation***<br><br>**Prohibited Cosmetic Ingredients** Bithional Vinyl chloride Certain halogenated salicylanilides Zirconium in aerosol products Chloroform Methylene chloride Chlorofluorocarbon propellants Prohibited cattle material  **CFR Citation** 21 CFR 700.11 21 CFR 700.14 21 CFR 700.15 21 CFR 700.16 21 CFR 700.18 21 CFR 700.19 21 CFR 700.23 21 CFR 700.27\n     \n     This talks about prohibited ingredients, showing the FDA's role in keeping your face safe, even if they don't approve labels! \n\n- Document title 2\n     \n     - > ***Contains Nonbinding Recommendations***<br><br>***Draft-Not for Implementation***<br><br>**Restricted Cosmetic Ingredients** Mercury compounds Hexachlorophene  **CFR Citation** 21 CFR 700.13 21 CFR 250.250\n     \n     This highlights restricted ingredients, which is relevant because it shows the FDA's focus on safety over pre-approval of labels! \n\n</markdown>	text	2025-02-23 22:22:31.162501-08	bot	Generate
224	5	1	4	Does FDA have a definition for the term "organic"?	text	2025-02-23 22:22:53.207401-08	user	\N
223	5	1	4	<markdown>\n\nI think you might be a regulatory consultant because the question is about law, regulation, or policy regarding product labeling. \n\nHere I provide the answer to your question: If you’ve got a product that’s both a drug and a cosmetic, it’s like a fancy two-in-one shampoo and conditioner, but with a twist! 🥳🎉 You gotta label it like it’s going to a fancy dress party! 🎭✨ \n\nSo, you need to make sure it’s clear what it does! If it’s gonna beautify you and also cure your dandruff, slap on that label saying it’s both! But remember, you can’t just throw on glitter and call it a day! 💅💖 You gotta follow the rules, which means labeling it as a cosmetic AND a drug. \n\n### References\n\n- Guidance - Cosmetics - Registration Listing-12-02-24\n     \n     - > ***Contains Nonbinding Recommendations***<br><br>• Cosmetic products that are intended to alter appearance for more than 24 hours under conditions of use that are customary or usual and removal by the consumer is not part of such conditions of use that are customary or usual.\n  \n      This explains that if a product alters appearance for more than 24 hours, it needs to be labeled properly to reflect its dual nature.\n  \n   - > **3. What is a food, drug, or cosmetic?**<br><br>The FD&C Act defines food, drug, and cosmetic in pertinent part as: <br><br>• Food means “(1) articles used for food or drink for man or other animals, (2) chewing gum, and (3) articles used for components of any such article” (section 201(f)); For example,\n  \n      This context provides the definitions that clarify how products are categorized, helping inform proper labeling.\n\n- Guidance-for-Industry---Complementary-and-Alternative-Medicine-Products-and-their-Regulation-by-the-Food-and-Drug-Administration-(PDF)\n\n     - > However, if the moisturizer’s intended use is also for the diagnosis, cure, mitigation, treatment, or prevention of disease, or to affect the structure or any function of the body, then it may also be subject to regulation as a drug.\n  \n      This context shows how a product can fall under both categories and the implications for labeling.\n\n</markdown>	text	2025-02-23 22:22:43.174739-08	bot	Generate
225	5	1	4	<markdown>\n\nI think you might be a regulatory consultant because the question is about law, regulation, or policy. \n\nHere I provide the answer to your question... Well, the FDA is like that friend who can't quite decide what "organic" means! 🌱 They don’t have a specific definition for "organic" when it comes to cosmetics. Like, they just kinda shrug and say, "Hey, if it smells good and makes you look fab, who cares?" 😂\n\nBut seriously, when it comes to food, they have some rules. But for cosmetics? It’s like a wild party where everyone can claim they’re organic without any official badge! 🎉 So, watch out for those sneaky labels, my friend! \n\n### References\n\n- Document title 1\n\n    - > "Guidance - Cosmetics - Registration Listing-12-02-24"\n    \n      This document outlines various cosmetic products but doesn’t provide a definition for "organic," indicating the FDA's lack of specific guidelines for organic cosmetics.\n  \n- Document title 2\n\n    - > "Guidance-for-Industry---Complementary-and-Alternative-Medicine-Products-and-their-Regulation-by-the-Food-and-Drug-Administration-(PDF)"\n    \n      This document discusses FDA authority but does not define "organic" in the context of cosmetics, showing the ambiguity in regulatory definitions. \n\n</markdown>	text	2025-02-23 22:22:59.627259-08	bot	Generate
