385	1	***Contains Nonbinding Recommendations***<br><br>the degree of purity, and \no <br><br>any known impurities or additives. \no <br><br>A thorough understanding of the details of the manufacturing process will help identify \nresidual additives and impurities, as well as certain other physical and chemical properties. \nA wide range of physical and chemical properties should be evaluated to help determine if a \nsubstance produced with nanotechnology is safe for the proposed use (Refs. 7, 13, 14, 15). \nProper characterization should include, as appropriate: <br><br>• \n• \n•  measurement of particle size and distribution, \naggregation and agglomeration characteristics, \nsurface chemistry, including: <br><br>zeta potential/surface charge, \no \nsurface coating, \no \nfunctionalization, and \no \ncatalytic activity \no \nmorphology including: \nshape, \nsurface area, \nsurface topology, and \ncrystallinity <br><br>• <br><br>o \no \no \no \nsolubility, \ndensity, \nstability, and \nporosity.  • \n• \n• \n• <br><br>Although a wide range of analytical techniques are available for measurement of \nphysicochemical properties of materials (Refs. 8, 9, 16), many of these methods have not \nbeen validated for the evaluation of nanomaterials in cosmetic products. Therefore, \nappropriate analytical methods suitable for the specific nanomaterial and the cosmetic \nproduct formulation should be chosen, and results obtained from such tests appropriately \ninterpreted and reported for adequate characterization of the material.	[0,1517)	[-0.4233651,-1.4176441,0.5628066,0.40754312,0.19632351,-0.06311269,0.024999376,-0.08984741,0.3545992,0.21611914,-0.13043116,-0.2849964,-0.41388515,0.2967778,-0.16315913,0.4809859,-0.036207475,-0.17929642,-0.21143505,-0.07822383,-0.45271257,0.08826336,-0.6034188,0.5069684,0.14646538,0.2129158,0.3438661,0.6015142,0.23447168,0.70035577,-0.43923315,0.1520418,-0.15147822,-0.1244693,-0.60811293,-0.24823296,-0.5282422,0.3156742,0.23277658,0.7897379,