531	1	7. “Biological Product” <br><br>Section 351(a)(1) of the PHS Act (42 U.S.C. 262(a)(1)) states, in part, that no <br><br>person “shall introduce or deliver for introduction into interstate commerce any \nbiological product” unless that product has an effective license and its package is plainly \nmarked with the product’s proper name, the name, address, and applicable license \nnumber of the biological product’s manufacturer, and the product’s expiration date. \nSection 351(a)(2) of the PHS Act gives us the authority to establish requirements for the \napproval, suspension, and revocation of biological product licenses. <br><br>Section 351(i) of the PHS Act defines “biological product” as “a virus, <br><br>therapeutic serum, toxin, antitoxin, vaccine, blood, blood component or derivative, \nallergenic product, or analogous product, or arsphenamine or derivative of arsphenamine \n(or any other trivalent organic arsenic compound), applicable to the prevention, \ntreatment, or cure of a disease or condition in human beings.” The term “virus” captures \na broad spectrum of microorganisms that cause an infectious disease and includes, but is \nnot limited to, filterable viruses, bacteria, rickettsia, fungi, and protozoa (see 21 CFR \n600.3(h)(1)).	[0,1276)	[0.03154829,-0.63874537,0.4605017,0.26383907,-0.6787012,-0.35639384,0.48090586,-0.542208,0.2293135,0.2756083,-0.117631085,-0.43483543,-0.07273353,0.057163294,-0.18302962,1.1599154,0.3687279,0.04934018,-0.57239103,-0.40024862,-0.14988254,-0.4675546,-0.28861907,-0.52799994,0.6407447,-0.23871453,0.45532873,0.7719708,0.6487332,0.37530687,-0.28207317,0.06132008,-0.0016662008,-0.18096419,-0.5372314,-0.2949014,-0.65444136,-0.062031385,0.15471345,0.38432676,-0.35682493,0.43350306,-0.3385293,0.44408187,-0.44340274,-0.24296127,-0.22226378,-0.019648096,-0.2708991,-0.47858244,-0.42766425,-0.49168047,0.474026,-0.45696363,-0.15919489,-0.077025875,1.252872,0.008419932,-0.34463543,0.2628905,-0.3339491,0.8208397,-0.9560791,-0.1944